# Reverse Syllogism and New-Style Questions

> Reverse syllogism gives you the conclusions first and asks which set of
> statements supports them — this lesson covers the elimination method and
> new-phrase patterns like "only a few" and "can never be."

[important]
Reverse syllogism rewards elimination, not construction. You rarely
build the statement set yourself — you test each option against the
conclusions and delete the ones that fail: one violated conclusion kills
an entire option set.
[/important]

## 1. What Reverse Syllogism Is

Classic form: statements given, conclusions given, pick the conclusions
that follow. **Reverse form:** conclusions given, four option sets of
statements given, pick the set from which those conclusions definitely
follow — a banking-exam staple, since guessing requires checking every
conclusion against every option.

## 2. The Method: Conclusions First

1. Draw the Venn picture demanded by each **conclusion** before looking
   at any option: an overlap for "some", total separation for "no".
2. Note what each conclusion *rules out* — your kill list.
3. Test each option set: the statements must make every conclusion
   definitely true.

[definition]
A **kill condition** is a fact that, if present in an option's statements,
makes some conclusion impossible — kill conditions delete options in
seconds without full diagrams.
[/definition]

## 3. Elimination Workflow Table

| Step | Action | Typical result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Translate conclusions into Venn requirements | two or three must-have pictures |
| 2 | Scan option (a): does it contradict any conclusion? | if yes, strike it off |
| 3 | Scan options (b), (c), (d) the same way | usually two die here |
| 4 | For survivors, draw the strictest statement (E or A) first, then check every conclusion | last survivor is the answer |

## 4. Worked Example with Four Options

**Conclusions:** I. Some cars are buses. II. No bus is a cycle.

```text
Kill list from I:  "No car/bus" statements die.
Kill list from II: "All/Some buses are cycles" die.
```

- **Option (a):** All cars are buses. All buses are cycles. — I survives,
  but II dies: buses and cycles overlap. **Eliminated.**
- **Option (b):** Some cars are buses. No bus is a cycle. — both
  conclusions stated outright. **Survives.**
- **Option (c):** All cars are cycles. Some cycles are buses. — II dies:
  some cycles being buses means a bus is a cycle. **Eliminated.**
- **Option (d):** No car is a bus. No bus is a cycle. — I dies: no cars
  are buses contradicts some cars being buses. **Eliminated.**

Answer: **option (b)** — the kill list deleted three options with no diagram at all.

[tip]
Keep the E-type ("No X is Y") at the top of your kill list — a single
No-statement contradicts the largest number of conclusions, so it
eliminates options fastest.
[/tip]

## 5. "Only a Few" Statements

Recent exams add the phrase **only a few**, stronger than "some."

| Statement | What it means |
| --- | --- |
| Some A are B | at least one A is a B (maybe all are) |
| Only a few A are B | some A are B AND some A are not B |

[definition]
**Only a few A are B** carries two commitments: at least one A is a B,
and at least one A is not a B. It sits strictly between "some" and "no."
[/definition]

**Worked case:** Only a few doctors are surgeons. "Some doctors are
surgeons" and "Some doctors are not surgeons" are both definitely true,
while "All doctors are surgeons" and "No doctor is a surgeon" are both
definitely **false** — each contradicted by half the statement.

## 6. "Can Never Be" and Other New Phrasings

| Phrase | Standard translation |
| --- | --- |
| A can never be B | No A is B — must be definitely true from the statements |
| A can be B | Some A being B is a possibility — true unless contradicted |
| At least some A are B | same as Some A are B |
| Unless A, B | if not A, then B — rewrite as: all non-A are B |

**Worked case:** Only a few pens are pencils. No pencil is a marker.
Conclusion: Pens can never be markers. Nothing separates the pen and
marker circles — the statements never mention them together — so the
conclusion **does not follow**.

[warning]
"Can never be" is the mirror of possibility: it demands the strongest
proof. A single arrangement where an A sits inside B destroys "A can
never be B" — treat it exactly like a No-conclusion.
[/warning]

## 7. Common Traps Table

| Trap | How it catches candidates | Defence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Partial truth | option (a) satisfies conclusion I but kills II | check every conclusion before accepting |
| Reversed conversion | option relies on All B are A from All A are B | recall the conversion table |
| "Only a few" read as "some" | ignores the guaranteed outsider | both parts must hold |
| Near-duplicate options | two options differ by one word | re-read the differing word slowly |

## 8. Practice Strategy and Timing

Budget **about two minutes per question**: 30 seconds for the conclusion
pictures and kill list, the rest for scanning options. If two options
remain alive, the E-type or "only a few" statement almost always breaks
the tie. If still stuck, mark and skip: a wrong guess costs the same.

## 9. Strategy Summary

| Situation | Move |
| --- | --- |
| Conclusions given, statements in options | draw conclusions first, build a kill list |
| An option contradicts one conclusion | eliminate the option immediately |
| "Only a few A are B" appears | read it as some are + some are not |
| "Can never be" appears | demand definite proof of total separation |
| Two options survive past two minutes | hunt for the E-type or only-a-few tie-breaker |

[example]
Exam method: **(1)** convert each conclusion into a must-have picture
and a kill list, **(2)** scan all four options against it — most die
in seconds, **(3)** for survivors, draw the strictest statement first,
**(4)** verify every conclusion, **(5)** accept the survivor.
[/example]